Explain concurrency vs parallelism?
1. Concurrency

Concurrency means multiple tasks make progress during overlapping periods of time, but they don't necessarily execute simultaneously.


Tasks can take turns executing.

One thread can handle multiple coroutines.

When one coroutine suspends, another can execute.
 

2.Parallelism means multiple tasks execute at exactly the same time.

Tasks run simultaneously.

Usually requires multiple threads and multiple CPU cores.

Different CPU cores can execute different tasks at the same time.



6.What is the structured concurrency?

Structured concurrency is a programming principle where coroutines are organized in a parent-child hierarchy (tree structure).

It ensures that coroutines have a clear lifecycle and that parent coroutines manage their child coroutines.

Parent coroutine: The coroutine that launches other coroutines.

Child coroutine: A coroutine launched inside another coroutine.

A parent manages the lifecycle of its children.

Parent failure normally cancels all its children.

If the parent encounters an uncaught exception, its children are cancelled.

The failure is propagated through the coroutine hierarchy.

The parent completes exceptionally.

